WEBVTT

1
00:00:44.727 --> 00:00:48.891
ANNA: When I was a kid, my mother told me
that I was a little piece of blue sky...

2
00:00:49.064 --> 00:00:53.728
...that came into this world
because she and Dad loved me so much.

3
00:00:56.071 --> 00:00:59.734
It was only later that I realized
that it wasn't exactly true.

4
00:01:04.246 --> 00:01:07.238
Most babies are coincidences.

5
00:01:07.416 --> 00:01:10.317
I mean, up in space
you got all these souls flying around...

6
00:01:10.486 --> 00:01:12.511
...Iooking for bodies to live in.

7
00:01:12.688 --> 00:01:16.351
Then, down here on Earth,
two people have sex or whatever...

8
00:01:16.525 --> 00:01:19.619
...and bam, coincidence.

9
00:01:23.332 --> 00:01:28.599
Sure, you hear all these stories about
how everyone plans these perfect families...

10
00:01:28.771 --> 00:01:30.602
...but the truth is that most babies...

11
00:01:30.773 --> 00:01:34.402
...are products of drunken evenings
and lack of birth control.

12
00:01:36.378 --> 00:01:38.278
They're accidents.

13
00:01:38.647 --> 00:01:43.243
Only people who have trouble
making babies actually plan for them.

14
00:01:50.058 --> 00:01:53.926
I, on the other hand,
am not a coincidence.

15
00:01:56.098 --> 00:01:58.032
I was engineered.

16
00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:01.095
Born for a particular reason.

17
00:02:03.539 --> 00:02:06.838
A scientist hooked up my mother's eggs
and my father's sperm...

18
00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:10.342
... to make a specific
combination of genes.

19
00:02:11.947 --> 00:02:14.609
He did it to save my sister's life.

20
00:02:19.288 --> 00:02:23.224
Sometimes I wonder what would have
happened if Kate had been healthy.

21
00:02:24.526 --> 00:02:26.858
I'd probably still be up in heaven
or wherever...

22
00:02:27.029 --> 00:02:30.465
...waiting to be attached to a body
down here on Earth.

23
00:02:32.734 --> 00:02:34.793
But coincidence or not...

24
00:02:35.704 --> 00:02:37.365
...I'm here.

25
00:02:48.584 --> 00:02:50.074
What can I do for you?

26
00:02:50.819 --> 00:02:53.811
Fourteen karat gold, hardly ever worn.

27
00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:59.994
That's my sister, Kate.

28
00:03:00.162 --> 00:03:01.959
She's dying.

29
00:03:02.898 --> 00:03:05.765
ANNA:
Montana? I don't get it.

30
00:03:06.268 --> 00:03:09.965
-lt looks like a whole bunch of nothing.
KATE: It is a whole bunch of nothing.

31
00:03:10.138 --> 00:03:13.039
-Just you and the big old sky.
-Why do you like it?

32
00:03:13.208 --> 00:03:16.939
I don't know. I like big things.
I like wide-open spaces.

33
00:03:17.379 --> 00:03:20.871
Besides,
it's the only place I've ever been.

34
00:03:22.451 --> 00:03:25.284
That's Mom and Aunt Kelly
making dinner.

35
00:03:25.454 --> 00:03:28.423
Since my sister got sick,
things have changed.

36
00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:32.686
Aunt Kelly only works part-time
and Mom quit her job as a lawyer.

37
00:03:32.861 --> 00:03:35.523
Her life now revolves around
keeping Kate alive.

38
00:03:35.697 --> 00:03:37.187
Cooking and cleaning.

39
00:03:37.366 --> 00:03:39.960
Everything steamed,
organic and germ-free.

40
00:03:40.135 --> 00:03:41.727
-Have a good night.
-Bye.

41
00:03:41.904 --> 00:03:44.498
I guess you could say
that we're a little dysfunctional.

42
00:03:44.673 --> 00:03:47.369
But everybody loves each other,
and we do the best we can.

43
00:03:47.542 --> 00:03:49.635
-That's not a word.
BRlAN: What about that language?

44
00:03:49.811 --> 00:03:51.836
-Nice shirt, sissy.
-Good, right?

45
00:03:52.014 --> 00:03:54.414
-Hey, Mom, you wanna see our routine?
-What routine?

46
00:03:54.583 --> 00:03:57.245
-Come on, Anna, let's do it.
-Hey, baby, what's your sign?

47
00:03:57.419 --> 00:03:58.579
-Cancer.
-You're a Cancer?

48
00:03:58.754 --> 00:04:00.779
-No, I'm a Leo--
KATE & BRlAN: But I have cancer.

49
00:04:00.956 --> 00:04:02.548
-It's not even funny.
-Yes, it is.

50
00:04:02.724 --> 00:04:05.716
JESSE: I agree with Dad.
-lt was pretty clever.

51
00:04:05.894 --> 00:04:06.952
-Boo.
GlRLS: Boo.

52
00:04:07.129 --> 00:04:08.994
JESSE:
It's not funny. It's not.

53
00:04:09.164 --> 00:04:10.825
Nice. Nice. These your kids?

54
00:04:10.999 --> 00:04:13.229
Yes, they are.
Question is, are they yours?

55
00:04:13.402 --> 00:04:15.962
You believe that?
Hey, sweetie, where's your locket?

56
00:04:16.138 --> 00:04:18.003
Oh, I didn't feel like wearing it today.

57
00:04:18.173 --> 00:04:19.936
-Eat, Kate.
-I'm eating.

58
00:04:20.108 --> 00:04:22.668
SARA: Is it not good?
-It's really good, Mom.

59
00:04:22.844 --> 00:04:23.936
BRlAN:
It's good to me.

60
00:04:24.112 --> 00:04:26.205
I have lentils in the fridge
if you want some.

61
00:04:26.381 --> 00:04:27.780
I'm fine, Mom.

62
00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:30.748
-You're not hungry?
-I bet she needs to breathe a little bit.

63
00:04:30.919 --> 00:04:34.150
-Maybe you should try to breathe a bit.
-Maybe I will a bit later.

64
00:04:34.323 --> 00:04:35.654
Gross.

65
00:04:35.824 --> 00:04:38.349
-Where are you going?
KATE: Bathroom, wanna come?

66
00:04:39.728 --> 00:04:41.753
SARA:
Will you breathe on me later?

67
00:05:09.057 --> 00:05:12.151
BRlAN: Having a child who is sick
is a full-time occupation.

68
00:05:15.063 --> 00:05:19.159
Sure, we still enjoy the usual
day-to-day happinesses of family life.

69
00:05:19.601 --> 00:05:23.469
Big house, great kids, beautiful wife.

70
00:05:23.905 --> 00:05:27.170
But beneath the exterior,
there are cracks...

71
00:05:27.342 --> 00:05:28.969
...resentments...

72
00:05:29.144 --> 00:05:32.636
...alliances that threaten
the very foundation of our lives...

73
00:05:32.814 --> 00:05:36.910
...as at any moment our whole world
could come tumbling down.

74
00:05:37.586 --> 00:05:42.250
BRlAN: I don't know when it started,
but probably around 11 it was 103.

75
00:05:42.858 --> 00:05:45.759
ANNA: Hey, what's going on?
SARA: Your sister's sick.

76
00:05:45.927 --> 00:05:48.361
She's been up all night with a fever.

77
00:05:49.398 --> 00:05:50.956
ANNA:
Kate?

78
00:05:52.434 --> 00:05:53.958
Kate?

79
00:06:06.548 --> 00:06:08.812
[COUGHlNG]

80
00:06:17.292 --> 00:06:18.384
ANNA:
Hey.

81
00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:21.393
JESSE:
You ready? Come on, let's go.

82
00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:33.671
Are you sure you want to do this?

83
00:06:37.345 --> 00:06:39.313
You know you're nuts, right?

84
00:06:42.918 --> 00:06:46.251
-I'll be right back.
-All right, I'll be waiting.

85
00:06:47.289 --> 00:06:49.689
CAMPBELL: When Anna Fitzgerald
first stepped into my office...

86
00:06:49.858 --> 00:06:52.053
...I thought she was selling
Girl Scout cookies.

87
00:06:53.628 --> 00:06:55.186
Thank you.

88
00:06:55.363 --> 00:06:58.560
-Oh, my gosh, it's really you.
-lt really is.

89
00:06:58.733 --> 00:07:00.462
" I'm Campbell Alexander.

90
00:07:00.635 --> 00:07:02.830
-I have a 90 percent success rate.
-Ninety-one.

91
00:07:03.004 --> 00:07:05.700
What can I do for you? "

92
00:07:05.874 --> 00:07:07.535
You've seen my commercials.

93
00:07:07.709 --> 00:07:10.109
All of them. I mean, that's why I'm here.

94
00:07:10.278 --> 00:07:13.111
-Hi.
-Please don't touch. Judge is a service dog.

95
00:07:13.281 --> 00:07:15.215
-What service?
-I have an iron lung...

96
00:07:15.383 --> 00:07:17.943
...and Judge helps me steer clear
of magnets.

97
00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:19.815
Please, sit down.

98
00:07:23.792 --> 00:07:26.317
-What is your name?
-Anna Fitzgerald.

99
00:07:26.495 --> 00:07:28.690
What can I do for you, Anna?

100
00:07:28.864 --> 00:07:30.957
That is so cool.

101
00:07:32.968 --> 00:07:36.199
I want to sue my parents
for the rights to my own body.

102
00:07:36.371 --> 00:07:37.861
Would you repeat that, please?

103
00:07:38.039 --> 00:07:41.202
I want to sue my parents
for the rights to my own body.

104
00:07:42.144 --> 00:07:45.739
My sister has leukemia. They're trying to
force me to give her my body parts.

105
00:07:45.914 --> 00:07:47.643
You're supposed to give her a kidney?

106
00:07:47.816 --> 00:07:50.250
She's been in renal failure
for months now.

107
00:07:50.418 --> 00:07:53.353
No one can force you to donate
if you don't want to, can they?

108
00:07:53.522 --> 00:07:54.648
They think they can.

109
00:07:54.823 --> 00:07:58.486
-I'm under 18, they're my legal guardians.
-They can't do that.

110
00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:00.423
That's what I want you to tell them...

111
00:08:00.595 --> 00:08:02.654
...they've been doing it to me
my whole life.

112
00:08:02.831 --> 00:08:05.322
I wouldn't even be alive
if Kate wasn't sick.

113
00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:06.797
I'm a designer baby.

114
00:08:07.235 --> 00:08:09.931
I was made in a dish
to be spare parts for Kate.

115
00:08:10.372 --> 00:08:11.600
You're kidding, right?

116
00:08:12.974 --> 00:08:14.908
CAMPBELL:
The kid wasn't lying.

117
00:08:15.076 --> 00:08:18.671
The doctors started taking things
from her the moment she was born.

118
00:08:18.847 --> 00:08:21.441
Cord blood as an infant,
white-cell transfusions...

119
00:08:21.616 --> 00:08:23.277
...bone marrow, lymphocytes...

120
00:08:23.451 --> 00:08:27.581
...injections to add more stem cells,
and then they took them too.

121
00:08:27.756 --> 00:08:29.656
But it was never enough.

122
00:08:32.027 --> 00:08:35.827
You do know what will happen if you don't
give your sister your kidney, don't you?

123
00:08:35.997 --> 00:08:37.988
Yeah, she'll die.

124
00:08:39.968 --> 00:08:43.961
So there's a little over $700 here.

125
00:08:44.139 --> 00:08:47.768
And I know that's not enough,
but it's all I have and I need your help.

126
00:08:50.245 --> 00:08:51.803
Please.

127
00:08:53.014 --> 00:08:55.312
You sure you want to
go through with this?

128
00:08:58.186 --> 00:08:59.915
Good for you.

129
00:09:05.193 --> 00:09:07.127
SARA:
It's hard to imagine now...

130
00:09:07.295 --> 00:09:10.526
...but there was a time
before all this happened...

131
00:09:10.966 --> 00:09:14.026
...when the kids were just kids,
and everyone was happy.

132
00:09:15.403 --> 00:09:17.234
SARA:
Hi, baby.

133
00:09:18.707 --> 00:09:20.470
-Where' s your sister?
-Sleeping.

134
00:09:20.642 --> 00:09:23.372
I tried poking her and everything.

135
00:09:26.615 --> 00:09:28.845
SARA:
Hey, Katie-Cat.

136
00:09:29.417 --> 00:09:32.682
Good morning, sweetie. Are you awake?

137
00:09:32.854 --> 00:09:34.253
How you doing?

138
00:09:34.422 --> 00:09:36.947
Rise and shine.

139
00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:43.388
SARA:
Anemia, right?

140
00:09:43.565 --> 00:09:47.092
-Kids her age don't get mono, do they?
WAYNE: Could be a virus.

141
00:09:47.269 --> 00:09:50.261
I'll have to draw some blood
and run a few tests.

142
00:09:53.275 --> 00:09:55.835
Kate's white cell count's
much lower than normal.

143
00:09:56.344 --> 00:09:58.505
-What's that mean?
-I don't know.

144
00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.274
She may have an autoimmune deficiency.

145
00:10:01.449 --> 00:10:03.781
Could just be a lab error.

146
00:10:06.588 --> 00:10:08.522
SARA:
Oncology?

147
00:10:09.057 --> 00:10:10.854
But that's cancer.

148
00:10:14.496 --> 00:10:16.088
WOMAN:
Mr. and Mrs. Fitzgerald?

149
00:10:17.432 --> 00:10:18.990
I'm lleana Farquad.

150
00:10:19.167 --> 00:10:21.635
KATE: Hello.
-Hi.

151
00:10:25.307 --> 00:10:27.639
So I took a look at Kate's CBC.

152
00:10:28.276 --> 00:10:30.244
Her white blood count is very low.

153
00:10:30.679 --> 00:10:35.139
She's also presenting with
12% promyelocytes and 5% blasts...

154
00:10:35.583 --> 00:10:38.074
...which does indicate
a leukemic syndrome.

155
00:10:38.253 --> 00:10:39.481
Leukemic?

156
00:10:39.921 --> 00:10:41.115
Cancer.

157
00:10:42.324 --> 00:10:44.519
I'll need a bone marrow
aspiration to confirm...

158
00:10:44.693 --> 00:10:49.096
...but it seems that Kate could have what
is called acute promyelocytic leukemia.

159
00:10:50.465 --> 00:10:52.126
I'm sorry.

160
00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:57.563
MAN:
Respiration's normal.

161
00:11:17.659 --> 00:11:19.024
BRlAN:
St. Joe's doesn't know shit.

162
00:11:20.929 --> 00:11:25.195
You remember when the chief's son was
playing with Jesse and broke his left arm?

163
00:11:25.367 --> 00:11:27.358
They put a cast on his right.

164
00:11:33.742 --> 00:11:36.074
I'm not gonna let her die.

165
00:11:37.178 --> 00:11:39.146
You know that, right?

166
00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:43.813
I'm not.

167
00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:13.504
Hello?

168
00:12:13.681 --> 00:12:14.875
KELLY:
We heard her coughing...

169
00:12:15.049 --> 00:12:17.279
...but she wouldn't
open the door. Your mom kicked it in.

170
00:12:17.452 --> 00:12:18.714
JESSE: Kicked the door down?
KELLY: Yeah.

171
00:12:18.887 --> 00:12:21.856
SARA: I haven't gotten in touch with them,
she's lost a lot of blood.

172
00:12:22.023 --> 00:12:24.082
We need to get
platelets and fluids into her.

173
00:12:24.259 --> 00:12:26.819
-I don't want her going into shock.
EMT 1: Her age and weight?

174
00:12:26.995 --> 00:12:30.658
SARA: Fifteen, she's about 90 pounds,
she's allergic to penicillin.

175
00:12:30.832 --> 00:12:32.231
EMT 2:
One-hundred over 68.

176
00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:33.765
EMT 1:
Okay, Base Camp, Rescue 11.

177
00:12:33.935 --> 00:12:36.096
We have a 15-year-old girl,
approximately 90 pounds.

178
00:12:36.271 --> 00:12:39.331
-BP is 100 over 68.
-Jesus Christ.

179
00:12:41.009 --> 00:12:43.443
EMT 2:
All right, let's get her up. Yeah.

180
00:12:47.482 --> 00:12:49.074
There you go.

181
00:12:54.422 --> 00:12:56.754
We're just gonna bring you downstairs.

182
00:13:04.098 --> 00:13:05.395
JESSE:
Nobody's saying anything...

183
00:13:05.567 --> 00:13:09.401
...but seeing everybody together
lets me know that this is serious.

184
00:13:10.672 --> 00:13:13.402
Our family is kind of disconnected.

185
00:13:13.641 --> 00:13:18.010
Dad's relatives are wealthy and distant,
and Mom's side drives her crazy.

186
00:13:18.179 --> 00:13:19.441
So besides Aunt Kelly...

187
00:13:20.081 --> 00:13:24.142
... we never really get to see anybody
except on holidays or disasters.

188
00:13:29.057 --> 00:13:30.547
NGUYEN:
Kate's leukemia is back.

189
00:13:30.992 --> 00:13:32.721
She's no longer remissing.

190
00:13:32.894 --> 00:13:36.455
CHANCE: We've looked at her smear,
and her leukemic cells are showing at 23%.

191
00:13:36.631 --> 00:13:39.099
-How many is bad?
-Any.

192
00:13:39.267 --> 00:13:40.291
TOMMY:
What about chemo?

193
00:13:40.468 --> 00:13:43.164
NGUYEN: It's an option,
but Kate doesn't seem to take it very well.

194
00:13:43.338 --> 00:13:45.670
And her cancer may be too far along.

195
00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:47.603
So you need more bone marrow?

196
00:13:47.775 --> 00:13:51.074
Yes, but the leukemia
isn't Kate's biggest problem now.

197
00:13:51.246 --> 00:13:53.441
She's lost the function of her kidneys.

198
00:13:54.315 --> 00:13:55.441
They've quit.

199
00:13:56.217 --> 00:13:57.844
They're gone.

200
00:14:00.221 --> 00:14:02.155
SARA: Not a match?
CHANCE: No.

201
00:14:02.323 --> 00:14:04.757
We're her parents, don't we have to be?

202
00:14:04.926 --> 00:14:08.760
Everyone inherits two sets
of chromosomes containing HLA genes.

203
00:14:08.930 --> 00:14:11.797
Unfortunately,
there's only a 1 in 200 chance...

204
00:14:11.966 --> 00:14:16.665
...that parents and their children will be
perfect histocompatible HLA matches.

205
00:14:17.639 --> 00:14:18.663
What about Jesse?

206
00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:20.998
I'm afraid not.

207
00:14:21.910 --> 00:14:25.812
It's possible that a donor will crop up
on the national bone-marrow registry.

208
00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:29.643
I thought you said getting a transplant
from an unrelated donor was dangerous.

209
00:14:29.817 --> 00:14:30.841
I did.

210
00:14:31.019 --> 00:14:34.648
But Kate's situation is time-sensitive,
and sometimes that's all we've got.

211
00:14:37.692 --> 00:14:40.354
I'd like to suggest something
completely off the record.

212
00:14:40.995 --> 00:14:44.226
Many times one sibling isn't a match,
but another is.

213
00:14:45.099 --> 00:14:47.363
Have you considered
having another child?

214
00:14:47.535 --> 00:14:49.526
Not to be forward...

215
00:14:49.704 --> 00:14:54.141
...but umbilical blood can be an incredibly
effective tool in treating leukemic patients.

216
00:14:54.576 --> 00:14:56.237
It's like a miracle.

217
00:14:56.411 --> 00:14:59.209
Well, how would you know
that the new child would be a match?

218
00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:01.940
-We could make sure of it.
-ln a test tube?

219
00:15:02.116 --> 00:15:06.815
Yes. With preimplantation genetic diagnosis,
it would be a 100 percent match.

220
00:15:07.322 --> 00:15:08.721
A donor child?

221
00:15:10.091 --> 00:15:11.854
It's not for everybody.

222
00:15:12.026 --> 00:15:14.551
And legally,
I can't even officially recommend it.

223
00:15:14.729 --> 00:15:18.062
But like I said,
cord blood would be invaluable.

224
00:15:18.232 --> 00:15:20.223
Well, we gotta do it.

225
00:15:21.736 --> 00:15:23.704
We gotta try.

226
00:15:24.706 --> 00:15:26.071
JESSE:
That was it.

227
00:15:26.407 --> 00:15:29.433
Grown in a dish,
they would have an in vitro child.

228
00:15:29.611 --> 00:15:34.071
A perfect chromosomal match
who would be Kate's genetic savior.

229
00:15:39.387 --> 00:15:42.379
SARA: Remember that time
where we strapped that fabric on her feet?

230
00:15:42.557 --> 00:15:44.320
Someone here to see you.

231
00:15:44.492 --> 00:15:47.620
MAN: Sara Fitzgerald?
-Yes.

232
00:15:49.797 --> 00:15:51.662
You've been served.

233
00:15:53.067 --> 00:15:54.830
KELLY:
What is that?

234
00:15:56.504 --> 00:15:58.768
SARA:
" Petition for medical emancipation by...."

235
00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:00.771
What does that mean?

236
00:16:01.442 --> 00:16:04.707
SARA: "The ability to make future medical
decisions independent of parents...

237
00:16:04.879 --> 00:16:08.315
...not to be forced to submit treatment
including donating her kidney."

238
00:16:08.483 --> 00:16:11.418
Anna, what is this? Is this from you?

239
00:16:11.586 --> 00:16:13.816
Yeah, I got a lawyer.

240
00:16:14.355 --> 00:16:16.550
SARA:
You're suing us?

241
00:16:16.724 --> 00:16:18.954
Well, honey, what the hell is going on?

242
00:16:19.127 --> 00:16:20.890
I don't want to do it anymore, Mom.

243
00:16:21.062 --> 00:16:24.327
You don't want to do it anymore?
That's it? You don't want to do it?

244
00:16:24.499 --> 00:16:25.659
No.

245
00:16:25.833 --> 00:16:27.960
Guess what?
Neither do I and neither does Kate.

246
00:16:28.136 --> 00:16:30.001
-Please.
-It's not like we have a choice.

247
00:16:30.171 --> 00:16:33.163
-That's the thing, I do. I do have a choice.
SARA: Really? Is that so?

248
00:16:33.341 --> 00:16:35.468
-Yeah.
-That's your sister. Have you forgotten?

249
00:16:35.643 --> 00:16:37.372
-No.
-Do you know what's gonna happen?

250
00:16:37.545 --> 00:16:40.378
Yeah. Believe it or not, Mom,
I actually thought about it.

251
00:16:40.548 --> 00:16:42.140
Sara.

252
00:16:44.352 --> 00:16:46.513
KELLY: Have you lost your mind?
SARA: What is she doing?

253
00:16:46.688 --> 00:16:49.680
KELLY: What the hell were you thinking?
SARA: What the hell is she thinking?

254
00:16:49.857 --> 00:16:51.085
I don't understand....

255
00:16:52.260 --> 00:16:54.228
MAN:
Let's hit it now, go.

256
00:17:02.804 --> 00:17:03.828
GUS:
Fitzgerald!

257
00:17:04.005 --> 00:17:07.065
Your wife called, she said to give her a call,
it's important. 911.

258
00:17:07.241 --> 00:17:09.334
-Just telling me now?
-I'm not your secretary.

259
00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:11.944
-Get your own frigging calls.
-Goddamn it, Gus.

260
00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:15.112
BRlAN:
All right, let's hear it. What's going on?

261
00:17:16.617 --> 00:17:17.777
Okay.

262
00:17:18.486 --> 00:17:22.286
Forget about the fact that the operation
is dangerous, or that it would hurt...

263
00:17:22.457 --> 00:17:25.290
...or that I might not want to have
something cut out of me.

264
00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:28.395
But if I only have one kidney,
then what happens to me?

265
00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:30.127
What if I need it?

266
00:17:30.298 --> 00:17:34.462
And am I really never allowed to play sports
or be a cheerleader or get pregnant?

267
00:17:34.635 --> 00:17:36.796
Can't drink.

268
00:17:37.972 --> 00:17:40.099
What if I just want to live a long time?

269
00:17:40.274 --> 00:17:42.174
Sweetheart,
you're gonna live a long time.

270
00:17:42.343 --> 00:17:44.573
Yeah? Then tell me this:

271
00:17:44.746 --> 00:17:47.374
What if the transplant doesn't work?
What then?

272
00:17:47.548 --> 00:17:50.381
-She's your sister.
-I know that! But I'm not like you, Mom!

273
00:17:50.551 --> 00:17:53.987
I see the other kids, I see what they do.
They go to parties, the beach.

274
00:17:54.155 --> 00:17:56.715
I don't understand
why you didn't say something earlier.

275
00:17:56.891 --> 00:17:58.756
When?
When should I talk to you about it?

276
00:17:58.926 --> 00:18:01.417
You're never home.
You leave me here with her.

277
00:18:01.596 --> 00:18:05.896
Excuse me? You've never had to do anything
you don't want to do, and you know that!

278
00:18:06.067 --> 00:18:08.126
I always wound up doing everything,
didn't l?

279
00:18:08.302 --> 00:18:09.860
-You spoiled shit!
-Stop yelling!

280
00:18:10.037 --> 00:18:11.095
BRlAN:
Everyone be quiet.

281
00:18:11.272 --> 00:18:13.900
We said she gets the table,
she gets the table.

282
00:18:14.976 --> 00:18:16.603
Go ahead.

283
00:18:18.346 --> 00:18:22.009
Remember how the doctor said
if I did the operation...

284
00:18:22.183 --> 00:18:24.708
...I would have to be careful
for the rest of my life?

285
00:18:24.886 --> 00:18:28.788
But I don't want to be careful.
Who wants to live like that?

286
00:18:28.956 --> 00:18:32.892
SARA: Anna, listen--
-I'm important too, Mom. I'm important too.

287
00:18:33.060 --> 00:18:35.187
-Hey, now--
-Let me go!

288
00:18:38.733 --> 00:18:41.167
BRlAN: Mom needs to cool off a bit.
She's a little upset.

289
00:18:41.335 --> 00:18:43.303
ANNA:
Yeah, I heard her.

290
00:18:43.471 --> 00:18:46.838
"Get her out of here.
I don't want to look at her face anymore."

291
00:18:49.610 --> 00:18:54.047
BRlAN: I looked at my daughter and
wondered how it got from there to here.

292
00:19:00.988 --> 00:19:03.320
MAN: Hey!
-Hi!

293
00:19:03.491 --> 00:19:05.857
[CHATTERlNG]

294
00:19:14.569 --> 00:19:16.867
-The truth, the truth.
-Okay.

295
00:19:22.043 --> 00:19:24.011
All right, so, what do you think?

296
00:19:24.178 --> 00:19:25.475
A bit more salt.

297
00:19:25.646 --> 00:19:27.409
A bit more.

298
00:19:40.294 --> 00:19:42.728
BRlAN: From the moment we decided
to genetically conceive...

299
00:19:42.897 --> 00:19:45.525
...I suppose this was
the eventual outcome.

300
00:19:45.967 --> 00:19:51.200
It was our fault. We went against nature
and this was our comeuppance.

301
00:19:53.140 --> 00:19:55.199
But have we really pushed her too hard?

302
00:19:55.376 --> 00:19:58.277
Have we forced her
into helping her sister?

303
00:19:59.714 --> 00:20:04.413
All those little encouragements
and rewards, were they real?

304
00:20:04.585 --> 00:20:07.520
Or did we just want what we wanted?

305
00:20:08.389 --> 00:20:11.085
She was so little when all this started.

306
00:20:11.959 --> 00:20:15.395
When did she start
wanting to make her own decisions?

307
00:20:15.863 --> 00:20:17.296
BRlAN: It's okay.
YOUNG ANNA: I want my daddy.

308
00:20:17.465 --> 00:20:20.730
-I want my mama! No, please! Mama!
BRlAN: It's okay. I know.

309
00:20:20.902 --> 00:20:22.802
YOUNG ANNA:
Mommy!

310
00:20:28.943 --> 00:20:31.309
BRlAN:
I guess the answer is now.

311
00:20:34.148 --> 00:20:36.048
SARA:
What do you mean, you don't know?

312
00:20:36.217 --> 00:20:38.185
BRlAN:
She won't talk.

313
00:20:40.054 --> 00:20:42.488
You know,
maybe she just wants to be considered.

314
00:20:42.657 --> 00:20:46.093
Take the credit that it's her decision,
I don't know.

315
00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:48.922
-Maybe it's not crazy.
SARA: What, you don't want her to do it?

316
00:20:49.096 --> 00:20:50.120
I didn't say that.

317
00:20:50.298 --> 00:20:52.994
But it's against her will,
so how does that work?

318
00:20:53.167 --> 00:20:55.499
-Do you hold her down, or do l?
SARA: Don't be dramatic.

319
00:20:55.670 --> 00:20:57.865
You gonna take her ankles,
I'll take her wrists?

320
00:20:58.039 --> 00:21:01.065
-She's not a baby. You just can't trick her.
-I know.

321
00:21:01.242 --> 00:21:02.607
So then what?

322
00:21:03.210 --> 00:21:04.973
-I don't know.
-You want to sedate her?

323
00:21:05.146 --> 00:21:07.842
Get handcuffs, tell her
we're going to get ice cream again?

324
00:21:08.015 --> 00:21:10.711
Because if we do,
every day for the rest of her life...

325
00:21:10.885 --> 00:21:14.685
...she's gonna look at us
like we forced her, like we used her.

326
00:21:14.956 --> 00:21:17.948
-And she'll be right.
-For chrissakes, Brian, I didn't do this.

327
00:21:18.125 --> 00:21:22.687
-Whose side are you on?
-Side. What sides? Are there sides now?

328
00:21:23.965 --> 00:21:26.058
She doesn't want to do it.

329
00:21:31.372 --> 00:21:32.862
GLORlA:
Your 11:00 appointment is here.

330
00:21:33.040 --> 00:21:35.474
-I don't have an 11:00.
GLORlA: Wait, you can't go in there.

331
00:21:35.643 --> 00:21:36.735
Get your hands off me.

332
00:21:36.911 --> 00:21:40.176
Mr. Alexander,
I'm Sara Fitzgerald, Anna's mother.

333
00:21:40.381 --> 00:21:43.475
-It's okay, Gloria.
-Are you sure? I don't mind calling security.

334
00:21:43.651 --> 00:21:44.811
CAMPBELL:
No, no. Thank you.

335
00:21:45.653 --> 00:21:47.484
What can I do for you, Mrs. Fitzgerald?

336
00:21:47.655 --> 00:21:51.113
The legal age for emancipation of a minor
is 14 in the state of California.

337
00:21:51.292 --> 00:21:54.955
It's the law, you might want to check it.
Anna's 11, you're three years early.

338
00:21:55.129 --> 00:21:57.222
-I'm aware, she's challenging.
-She can't.

339
00:21:57.398 --> 00:22:00.526
-She's too young to stand.
-I'm filing for her as guardian ad litem.

340
00:22:00.701 --> 00:22:02.566
As what? A family independence agency?

341
00:22:02.737 --> 00:22:05.604
I have 15 years as a volunteer member
of the ACLU...

342
00:22:05.773 --> 00:22:10.710
...in addition to which I have this power
of attorney signed by your daughter.

343
00:22:16.250 --> 00:22:18.741
This will never hold up.
It's not even legal.

344
00:22:18.919 --> 00:22:22.446
Anna doesn't want to do it anymore.
And 11 years old or not, she has rights.

345
00:22:22.623 --> 00:22:25.820
And so long as she wants to move forward,
I am going to help her.

346
00:22:25.993 --> 00:22:28.723
Why? What's your interest?
This isn't a case for you.

347
00:22:28.896 --> 00:22:30.659
-There's no money.
-What's my interest?

348
00:22:30.831 --> 00:22:33.265
Eight hospitalizations in 11 years...

349
00:22:33.434 --> 00:22:36.801
...six catheterizations,
two bone-marrow aspirations...

350
00:22:36.971 --> 00:22:39.439
-...two stem-cell purges.
-She was helping her sister.

351
00:22:39.607 --> 00:22:43.168
Not to mention the side effects,
including bleeding, infections, bruising.

352
00:22:43.344 --> 00:22:46.006
Filgrastim shots.
Those are growth hormones, am I correct?

353
00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:49.081
-Something like that.
-Drugs for nausea, opiates for pain...

354
00:22:49.250 --> 00:22:52.447
...Ambien for sleep. Not exactly
the proper medication for a preteen.

355
00:22:52.620 --> 00:22:55.282
Every procedure
had its risks and complications.

356
00:22:55.456 --> 00:22:57.924
Anna understood that,
she was okay with it.

357
00:22:58.092 --> 00:22:59.491
-Really?
-Yeah.

358
00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:01.491
At 5 years old.

359
00:23:03.030 --> 00:23:05.931
Oh, my God, you're good.
You're really good.

360
00:23:06.100 --> 00:23:09.035
You know, I've seen your commercials,
right? I mean, who hasn't?

361
00:23:09.203 --> 00:23:11.728
I always thought you were
some headline-seeking hack...

362
00:23:11.906 --> 00:23:13.498
...but you have real talent.

363
00:23:13.674 --> 00:23:16.404
You almost had me believing
that you cared about Anna.

364
00:23:16.577 --> 00:23:19.478
Funny, I was about to say
the same thing to you.

365
00:23:22.016 --> 00:23:24.541
Mrs. Fitzgerald,
did you ever say to yourself:

366
00:23:24.718 --> 00:23:27.551
" Maybe I'm wrong,
maybe I took it a little too far? "

367
00:23:28.556 --> 00:23:30.581
I'll see you in court.

368
00:23:32.793 --> 00:23:34.317
SARA:
You gotta get up.

369
00:23:34.495 --> 00:23:36.588
You haven't been out of that bed
in two weeks.

370
00:23:36.764 --> 00:23:38.732
KATE: I'm tired.
-You can do it.

371
00:23:38.899 --> 00:23:40.890
BRlAN: It's a beautiful day outside.
KATE: No.

372
00:23:41.068 --> 00:23:42.899
It's sunny. It's gonna be good for you.

373
00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:45.265
KATE: I'm too sick.
SARA: You're not too sick.

374
00:23:45.439 --> 00:23:48.135
You're depressed.
I'm not gonna feed you antidepressants...

375
00:23:48.309 --> 00:23:50.903
...they're gonna just make you more numb.
Now, get up.

376
00:23:51.078 --> 00:23:52.739
-No!
BRlAN: Hey, baby?

377
00:23:52.913 --> 00:23:56.849
-You want to tell me what's really wrong?
-I'm tired. Don't you get that?

378
00:23:57.017 --> 00:23:59.747
-I'm sick, and I'm tired, and I'm ugly.
BRlAN: Stop it.

379
00:23:59.920 --> 00:24:02.946
KATE: Don't you dare tell me
that I'm beautiful, because I'm not.

380
00:24:03.390 --> 00:24:06.882
Don't you dare tell me that nobody's
gonna stare at me, because they will.

381
00:24:07.061 --> 00:24:09.120
SARA: Oh, God.
-I'm a freak.

382
00:24:09.296 --> 00:24:10.524
BRlAN:
Come here.

383
00:24:10.698 --> 00:24:12.996
-Okay, that's it.
BRlAN: It's okay.

384
00:24:16.670 --> 00:24:18.968
[RAZOR BUZZlNG]

385
00:24:24.712 --> 00:24:26.373
Mom?

386
00:24:38.425 --> 00:24:40.655
-[MOUTHlNG] Thanks, Mom.
-[MOUTHlNG] Mom, Mom.

387
00:25:36.617 --> 00:25:37.982
KATE:
This is it.

388
00:25:40.821 --> 00:25:43.312
I know I'm going to die now.

389
00:25:46.961 --> 00:25:49.623
I suppose I've always known that.

390
00:25:51.832 --> 00:25:54.130
I just never knew when.

391
00:26:00.074 --> 00:26:03.703
And I'm okay with it. Really.

392
00:26:04.745 --> 00:26:07.714
I don't mind my disease killing me.

393
00:26:09.650 --> 00:26:11.743
But it's killing my family too.

394
00:26:18.259 --> 00:26:21.456
While everyone was so worried
about my blood counts...

395
00:26:21.629 --> 00:26:25.326
...they barely even noticed
that Jesse was dyslexic.

396
00:26:27.167 --> 00:26:30.659
Look at this place.
You see all these kids around here?

397
00:26:30.904 --> 00:26:35.807
They got a football team,
this Olympic-style swimming pool.

398
00:26:35.976 --> 00:26:39.002
-Horses.
-They got horses.

399
00:26:39.713 --> 00:26:42.944
Massive playground here.
Look at that Tarzan rope into a lake.

400
00:26:43.117 --> 00:26:46.416
There's kids everywhere.
You're gonna have a great time there.

401
00:26:47.588 --> 00:26:49.021
What?

402
00:26:49.990 --> 00:26:53.426
Come on, you know this is gonna be
like a vacation for you.

403
00:26:54.094 --> 00:26:56.392
I'll try harder.

404
00:26:57.097 --> 00:26:59.031
It's not about that.

405
00:26:59.800 --> 00:27:01.461
Hey, look at me.

406
00:27:03.237 --> 00:27:07.105
You know how sometimes when you
see things, they are a little mixed up?

407
00:27:07.274 --> 00:27:09.902
This place is special because
they have teachers there...

408
00:27:10.077 --> 00:27:14.377
...that will show you
how to straighten it out.

409
00:27:15.082 --> 00:27:17.550
-Okay.
-And I can't do that.

410
00:27:17.718 --> 00:27:18.742
Okay.

411
00:27:19.353 --> 00:27:21.981
You're gonna get so good at math.

412
00:27:22.156 --> 00:27:25.648
-It's gonna be a year, that's it, all right?
-Just till your grades are up.

413
00:27:27.027 --> 00:27:29.359
If you don't like it, you come right home.

414
00:27:30.898 --> 00:27:33.594
-Okay.
-All right.

415
00:27:39.239 --> 00:27:41.332
KATE:
I'm sorry, Jesse.

416
00:27:43.043 --> 00:27:47.412
I'm sorry I took all the attention when
you were the one who needed it the most.

417
00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:03.860
Dad,
I know I took your first love from you.

418
00:28:06.533 --> 00:28:09.400
I only hope that one day,
you get her back.

419
00:28:16.276 --> 00:28:19.177
Mom, you gave up everything for me.

420
00:28:19.346 --> 00:28:24.181
Your work, your marriage,
your entire life...

421
00:28:24.351 --> 00:28:27.149
...just to fight my battles for me
every single day.

422
00:28:30.758 --> 00:28:33.090
I'm sorry you couldn't win.

423
00:28:35.863 --> 00:28:39.924
And to my baby sis,
who was always so very little...

424
00:28:42.669 --> 00:28:45.229
...I'm sorry I let them hurt you.

425
00:28:47.474 --> 00:28:50.341
I'm sorry I didn't take care of you.

426
00:28:51.478 --> 00:28:54.504
It was supposed to be
the other way around.

427
00:28:56.517 --> 00:28:58.985
-Hey.
-Hi.

428
00:29:00.721 --> 00:29:01.813
I got you something.

429
00:29:08.162 --> 00:29:10.562
-ls that me?
-Mm-hm.

430
00:29:10.998 --> 00:29:13.125
I'm not done with it, but....

431
00:29:27.080 --> 00:29:30.106
-De Salvo? I thought she was on leave.
-She was, she's back.

432
00:29:30.284 --> 00:29:33.344
Your Honor, clearly Miss Morangez
is afraid for her life.

433
00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:37.081
At this time, the plaintiff requests
a continuation on the restraining order.

434
00:29:37.257 --> 00:29:39.054
BAlLlFF:
Stand, please.

435
00:29:40.260 --> 00:29:43.559
DE SALVO:
Mr. Morangez, by order of this court...

436
00:29:43.730 --> 00:29:48.292
...you are no longer allowed
within 1000 yards...

437
00:29:48.902 --> 00:29:51.666
...of Mrs. Morangez or her residence.

438
00:29:51.839 --> 00:29:54.467
You understand
how far a thousand yards is?

439
00:29:54.641 --> 00:29:57.166
CAMPBELL: Judge De Salvo
had a very public nervous breakdown...

440
00:29:57.344 --> 00:30:01.542
...after her 12-year-old daughter
was killed by a drunk driver.

441
00:30:01.715 --> 00:30:05.151
She had taken a six-month leave of absence
to deal with her grief...

442
00:30:05.319 --> 00:30:08.345
...and this was the first time
I'd seen her back in court.

443
00:30:08.522 --> 00:30:10.149
DE SALVO:
You're going to jail.

444
00:30:10.324 --> 00:30:13.054
-Okay, next case, please.
MAN: Thank you, Your Honor.

445
00:30:14.461 --> 00:30:15.951
BAlLlFF:
Four-eight-five-seven-two.

446
00:30:16.396 --> 00:30:19.331
I'd like to see counsel in my chambers,
please.

447
00:30:24.104 --> 00:30:26.698
DE SALVO: Good afternoon.
SARA: Good afternoon.

448
00:30:27.074 --> 00:30:28.439
What's with the dog?

449
00:30:28.609 --> 00:30:30.008
He's a service dog, Your Honor.

450
00:30:30.177 --> 00:30:32.202
DE SALVO:
Well, please make sure he behaves himself.

451
00:30:32.379 --> 00:30:34.847
-I just had these carpets cleaned.
CAMPBELL: Yes, ma'am.

452
00:30:35.015 --> 00:30:37.677
Good to see you, Sara.
I wasn't aware you were practicing.

453
00:30:37.851 --> 00:30:41.912
I wasn't planning to, Your Honor,
but the complainant is my daughter.

454
00:30:43.156 --> 00:30:44.487
What's this about, counselor?

455
00:30:44.658 --> 00:30:48.594
Mrs. Fitzgerald's youngest child wishes to
be medically emancipated from her parents.

456
00:30:48.762 --> 00:30:51.230
There's no legal definition
of medical emancipation...

457
00:30:51.398 --> 00:30:53.025
...not in the state of California.

458
00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:56.328
It's one of those New Age terms
that Mr. Alexander has latched onto.

459
00:30:56.503 --> 00:30:59.870
Yes, I'm well aware
of Mr. Alexander's reputation.

460
00:31:00.040 --> 00:31:02.008
He once tried to sue God
in my courtroom.

461
00:31:02.175 --> 00:31:03.267
Did sue, Your Honor.

462
00:31:03.443 --> 00:31:05.638
Jerome Dylan v.
the Diocese of California.

463
00:31:05.812 --> 00:31:08.303
-And I won that one.
-I remember. So?

464
00:31:08.482 --> 00:31:10.541
So Mr. Alexander
wants you to change the law.

465
00:31:10.717 --> 00:31:13.447
An 11-year-old can't be emancipated,
at least not legally.

466
00:31:13.620 --> 00:31:15.679
My client is not seeking
to be emancipated.

467
00:31:15.856 --> 00:31:18.188
She loves her parents
and wants to live at home.

468
00:31:18.358 --> 00:31:20.087
She doesn't wanna be
cut open anymore.

469
00:31:20.260 --> 00:31:24.424
Yes, I read the complaint.
What is it exactly that you're after?

470
00:31:24.598 --> 00:31:26.623
Limited termination of parental rights.

471
00:31:26.800 --> 00:31:29.325
DE SALVO:
All right. Very smart.

472
00:31:29.503 --> 00:31:31.869
Very creative.
I assume you have precedent?

473
00:31:32.039 --> 00:31:33.700
Yes, Planned Parenthood v. Danforth.

474
00:31:33.874 --> 00:31:37.332
Mature minor doctrine:
Smith v. Seibly. Agrello v. Florida.

475
00:31:37.511 --> 00:31:39.502
And defense is moving
for summary dismissal?

476
00:31:39.680 --> 00:31:41.170
SARA:
Yes. Today, if possible.

477
00:31:41.348 --> 00:31:44.283
Anna's too young to make a decision
of this size on her own.

478
00:31:44.451 --> 00:31:46.442
She doesn't understand
what it is she wants.

479
00:31:46.620 --> 00:31:50.556
She's 11 years old.
She changes her mind every five minutes.

480
00:31:50.724 --> 00:31:53.215
You know how young girls can be.

481
00:31:56.897 --> 00:31:59.661
Oh, my God, I'm so sorry. I....

482
00:32:02.269 --> 00:32:03.566
Mr. Alexander?

483
00:32:03.737 --> 00:32:06.262
The best interests of the child,
Your Honor.

484
00:32:06.440 --> 00:32:09.000
All right.
Well, I need to see Anna. Is she here?

485
00:32:09.176 --> 00:32:11.508
SARA: Yes, but I was really hoping
to keep her out of it.

486
00:32:11.678 --> 00:32:15.273
Keep her out of it? Mrs. Fitzgerald,
you just told me your daughter...

487
00:32:15.449 --> 00:32:17.940
...doesn't fully understand
what she wants.

488
00:32:18.118 --> 00:32:20.177
If you want a decision today...

489
00:32:20.354 --> 00:32:23.619
...I need to find that out. Do you
have a problem with that, counselor?

490
00:32:23.790 --> 00:32:27.157
-None whatsoever.
-All right. Well, please send her in.

491
00:32:28.495 --> 00:32:30.520
CAMPBELL:
Thank you, Your Honor.

492
00:32:31.331 --> 00:32:32.958
And Sara?

493
00:32:34.968 --> 00:32:37.596
I'm awfully sorry about Kate.

494
00:32:53.186 --> 00:32:54.710
Thank you.

495
00:32:55.989 --> 00:32:57.115
Hi.

496
00:32:58.258 --> 00:32:59.282
Hi.

497
00:33:02.429 --> 00:33:04.829
You can sit down if you like.

498
00:33:07.601 --> 00:33:09.796
Want something to drink?

499
00:33:10.103 --> 00:33:12.128
I've got...

500
00:33:12.506 --> 00:33:17.170
...7UP, Dr. Pepper or apple juice.

501
00:33:17.344 --> 00:33:20.404
-7UP, please.
-7UP.

502
00:33:23.717 --> 00:33:25.446
Thank you.

503
00:33:32.426 --> 00:33:35.361
-So you know why you're here?
-Yup.

504
00:33:35.529 --> 00:33:37.190
Pretty scary stuff, huh?

505
00:33:38.465 --> 00:33:41.832
Well, I don't really like everyone
being mad at me.

506
00:33:42.202 --> 00:33:45.603
I meant having to
take care of your sister all the time.

507
00:33:45.772 --> 00:33:48.764
No, I don't mind. I like it.

508
00:33:49.242 --> 00:33:50.971
You guys get along okay?

509
00:33:51.144 --> 00:33:53.374
Of course, she's my sister.

510
00:33:54.848 --> 00:33:59.615
But all those operations,
it's gotta be pretty crazy, right?

511
00:34:01.722 --> 00:34:03.781
KATE: No, no, no, stop, stop, stop!
ANNA: Okay, okay.

512
00:34:03.957 --> 00:34:07.757
Quietly, girls.
This is a hospital, this is not a sorority.

513
00:34:07.928 --> 00:34:10.123
-I need a urine sample.
-But I don't have to go.

514
00:34:10.297 --> 00:34:12.527
-Well, drink something.
-And I'm not thirsty.

515
00:34:12.699 --> 00:34:15.463
Listen to me, young lady,
I don't like a lot of back talk.

516
00:34:15.635 --> 00:34:19.537
Drink, don't drink, I don't care.
But you just better fill it.

517
00:34:23.343 --> 00:34:25.402
-What a bitch.
-Right?

518
00:34:25.579 --> 00:34:27.604
I'll fill it.

519
00:34:28.482 --> 00:34:30.313
-Nice and warm.
-No.

520
00:34:32.853 --> 00:34:35.651
[BEEPlNG]

521
00:34:37.090 --> 00:34:39.957
-You ready?
KATE: All set.

522
00:34:40.727 --> 00:34:42.456
Oh, wait a minute.

523
00:34:42.629 --> 00:34:45.189
Looks a little cloudy.
I think I should filter it again.

524
00:34:50.403 --> 00:34:52.564
Much better. What do you think?

525
00:34:53.540 --> 00:34:54.939
You are disgusting.

526
00:34:57.110 --> 00:34:58.941
And so are you!

527
00:35:05.085 --> 00:35:08.111
Look at me, crying like an idiot.

528
00:35:08.989 --> 00:35:11.048
You're not an idiot.

529
00:35:12.425 --> 00:35:15.155
I see idiots every day.

530
00:35:15.629 --> 00:35:17.529
You're not even close.

531
00:35:20.801 --> 00:35:22.291
You okay?

532
00:35:22.469 --> 00:35:23.766
Where did you go?

533
00:35:44.291 --> 00:35:47.021
-Can I ask you something?
-Shoot.

534
00:35:50.730 --> 00:35:52.823
What was it like when she died?

535
00:35:54.134 --> 00:35:55.567
Who, Dina?

536
00:35:55.735 --> 00:35:59.227
Yeah, I mean, what did it feel like?

537
00:36:18.124 --> 00:36:20.058
I'm sorry.

538
00:36:20.460 --> 00:36:22.826
I shouldn't have said anything.

539
00:36:23.830 --> 00:36:26.128
Sometimes I just think things
and then say them.

540
00:36:30.704 --> 00:36:32.535
Did you know Dina?

541
00:36:32.706 --> 00:36:33.968
No.

542
00:36:34.140 --> 00:36:36.301
-No?
-She was older than me.

543
00:36:36.476 --> 00:36:41.345
I mean, I saw her on the TV and everything,
but I didn't know her.

544
00:36:41.514 --> 00:36:43.175
Yeah, you're....

545
00:36:43.750 --> 00:36:45.274
Yeah.

546
00:36:46.519 --> 00:36:49.716
-I'm real sorry.
-Don't be.

547
00:36:51.191 --> 00:36:52.715
There's no shame in dying.

548
00:36:57.664 --> 00:36:59.495
BAlLlFF:
All rise.

549
00:37:06.506 --> 00:37:09.134
I am going to rule against
summary dismissal.

550
00:37:09.309 --> 00:37:12.972
Please clear my calendar and schedule
a hearing for first thing Friday morning.

551
00:37:13.146 --> 00:37:14.170
Thank you.

552
00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:24.848
Hello?

553
00:38:42.469 --> 00:38:45.632
KATE: She's usually blowing me off,
but eventually....

554
00:38:45.805 --> 00:38:48.137
Girl, you need a day at the spa.

555
00:38:48.308 --> 00:38:51.800
-Yeah, I don't see that happening--
-The beautician, the mani, the pedi...

556
00:38:51.978 --> 00:38:54.845
-...the whole thing.
-Hello, Kate. One to 10, how's your pain?

557
00:38:55.015 --> 00:38:57.176
-Six.
CHANCE: Six?

558
00:38:57.350 --> 00:38:59.910
-All right, let's take care of that.
SARA: Got a minute?

559
00:39:00.086 --> 00:39:02.554
She's feeling pretty good today.

560
00:39:03.890 --> 00:39:08.122
Sara, these lucid moments
are gonna get briefer and further apart.

561
00:39:08.795 --> 00:39:13.027
Kate's in system failure now.
Body defenses are at zero.

562
00:39:13.199 --> 00:39:16.657
The fevers Kate's been getting,
it's infection from the dialysis.

563
00:39:17.103 --> 00:39:20.129
And it's not just in her arm or leg,
it's in her blood.

564
00:39:20.306 --> 00:39:21.830
I wanna see how.

565
00:39:22.008 --> 00:39:24.738
I'm sorry, Sara, this is it.

566
00:39:24.911 --> 00:39:26.811
We're at the end.

567
00:39:27.347 --> 00:39:29.110
Dr. Chance?

568
00:39:31.785 --> 00:39:33.912
What is it, princess?

569
00:39:41.361 --> 00:39:42.726
No good, huh?

570
00:39:45.632 --> 00:39:47.099
No.

571
00:39:49.969 --> 00:39:51.834
How long?

572
00:39:53.873 --> 00:39:57.138
It's hard to say.

573
00:39:57.310 --> 00:40:00.006
But if nothing changes...

574
00:40:01.981 --> 00:40:04.040
...not too long.

575
00:40:04.517 --> 00:40:06.144
SARA:
"Not too long"?

576
00:40:06.319 --> 00:40:07.650
Not too long what?

577
00:40:09.022 --> 00:40:10.853
-Will it hurt?
CHANCE: No.

578
00:40:12.192 --> 00:40:14.422
I'll make sure of that.

579
00:40:15.795 --> 00:40:18.787
Listen, I don't wanna hear talk like that,
okay, honey?

580
00:40:18.965 --> 00:40:22.833
You just stay strong enough
for surgery, okay?

581
00:40:23.002 --> 00:40:24.765
Okay, Mom.

582
00:40:25.138 --> 00:40:26.696
CHANCE:
Sara, let's take a walk.

583
00:40:31.711 --> 00:40:34.145
Let me introduce you to someone.

584
00:40:34.948 --> 00:40:37.075
This is Miss Swearingen,
our home health aide.

585
00:40:37.250 --> 00:40:38.911
-Hi, how are you?
-Good, how are you?

586
00:40:39.085 --> 00:40:40.347
I'm good, thank you.

587
00:40:40.520 --> 00:40:42.715
Have you spoken to
the Make-A-Wish people?

588
00:40:45.758 --> 00:40:47.623
What is this?

589
00:40:47.794 --> 00:40:49.853
What is this, the "quality of life" speech?

590
00:40:50.029 --> 00:40:52.827
Mrs. Fitzgerald, you might want to consider
taking Kate home.

591
00:40:52.999 --> 00:40:54.398
-No.
-Making her comfortable...

592
00:40:54.567 --> 00:40:56.592
-...managing her pain.
-No hospices.

593
00:40:56.769 --> 00:40:59.203
What? You think we should
take Kate home to die?

594
00:40:59.372 --> 00:41:02.034
What do you want me to say, Sara?
It's an option.

595
00:41:02.208 --> 00:41:05.905
Look, I know you don't listen to anybody,
but it's our job to tell you.

596
00:41:06.079 --> 00:41:09.708
Death is a normal process of life.
You need to acknowledge that.

597
00:41:09.883 --> 00:41:13.011
-No, I don't. Who is this broad?
-Have you spoken to Kate?

598
00:41:13.186 --> 00:41:15.882
-You know what she wants?
-I don't care what anybody wants.

599
00:41:16.055 --> 00:41:18.319
-We're doing the operation.
-What operation, Sara?

600
00:41:18.491 --> 00:41:20.220
You have an unwilling donor.

601
00:41:20.393 --> 00:41:23.487
The hospital won't even allow it
anymore without a court order.

602
00:41:23.663 --> 00:41:25.722
You think we don't know
what's going on here?

603
00:41:25.899 --> 00:41:29.164
Your daughter is dying,
and you might want to spend...

604
00:41:29.335 --> 00:41:31.599
-...some quality time with her.
-Lookit, sister--

605
00:41:31.771 --> 00:41:34.171
[lNAUDlBLE DlALOGUE]

606
00:41:42.782 --> 00:41:44.306
KELLY:
I did it.

607
00:41:44.484 --> 00:41:46.952
They look wonderful. Don't touch them.

608
00:41:47.120 --> 00:41:49.816
I gotta go. Going to work.

609
00:41:49.989 --> 00:41:52.253
-Bye.
-Bye. I'll be back.

610
00:42:32.465 --> 00:42:33.489
KATE:
Hey, Mom?

611
00:42:33.666 --> 00:42:37.193
Do you know that Minnesota is known
as The Land of Ten Thousand Lakes?

612
00:42:37.637 --> 00:42:40.105
Well, Montana's got, like,
a million times that.

613
00:42:40.273 --> 00:42:42.173
No, I didn't know that.

614
00:42:46.646 --> 00:42:48.136
Hi.

615
00:42:49.182 --> 00:42:50.649
Hello.

616
00:42:54.420 --> 00:42:56.183
What are you here for?

617
00:42:58.091 --> 00:42:59.388
The free cocktails.

618
00:43:00.426 --> 00:43:02.553
KATE:
Right. Happy hour.

619
00:43:04.831 --> 00:43:06.992
I'm Taylor. AML.

620
00:43:07.667 --> 00:43:10.261
Kate. APL.

621
00:43:10.436 --> 00:43:12.563
-A rarity.
-Yeah, right?

622
00:43:13.172 --> 00:43:15.834
TAYLOR: You're in remission?
KATE: Today, anyway.

623
00:43:17.110 --> 00:43:19.271
-Chemo?
-Yeah.

624
00:43:24.217 --> 00:43:28.210
So, what do you do
when you're not here at the hospital?

625
00:43:29.555 --> 00:43:31.147
KATE:
Nothing.

626
00:43:33.026 --> 00:43:35.995
Just wait for something
that makes me come back.

627
00:43:38.097 --> 00:43:41.089
Well, then maybe we could
hang together sometime?

628
00:43:42.135 --> 00:43:43.625
Yeah.

629
00:43:44.070 --> 00:43:45.537
Okay.

630
00:43:46.773 --> 00:43:48.798
Can I get your phone number?

631
00:43:50.743 --> 00:43:52.142
Okay.

632
00:44:07.694 --> 00:44:09.218
Thanks.

633
00:44:09.929 --> 00:44:11.328
Thanks, Mom.

634
00:44:12.131 --> 00:44:13.428
I'm Taylor, by the way.

635
00:44:13.866 --> 00:44:16.994
Sara Fitzgerald. Nice to meet you.

636
00:44:17.570 --> 00:44:20.630
ALlCE:
Okay, Taylor. You're out of here.

637
00:44:23.176 --> 00:44:26.737
555...

638
00:44:27.980 --> 00:44:31.677
...0173.

639
00:44:37.724 --> 00:44:39.589
Well, I gotta go.

640
00:44:40.026 --> 00:44:41.823
I'll call you, Kate.

641
00:44:48.267 --> 00:44:51.532
KATE: So, what do you think?
-Girl, that boy is fine.

642
00:44:51.704 --> 00:44:53.865
I know, right?
So do you think he'll call?

643
00:44:54.040 --> 00:44:55.598
I don't know, baby, l....

644
00:44:55.775 --> 00:44:57.675
[PHONE RlNGlNG]

645
00:45:07.420 --> 00:45:08.751
Hello?

646
00:45:08.921 --> 00:45:12.721
TAYLOR: Okay, I just wanted to make sure
you didn't give me a bogus number.

647
00:45:14.594 --> 00:45:18.325
SARA: The radiation,
which ultimately put Kate into remission...

648
00:45:18.498 --> 00:45:21.467
...worked its magic by wearing her down.

649
00:45:22.135 --> 00:45:26.765
Taylor Ambrose,
a drug of an entirely different sort...

650
00:45:26.939 --> 00:45:30.841
...worked his magic by building her up.

651
00:45:31.010 --> 00:45:33.843
[MUTED DlALOGUE]

652
00:46:09.849 --> 00:46:11.612
SARA: I don't think that we can
actually make it tomorrow...

653
00:46:11.784 --> 00:46:12.808
[PHONE RlNGlNG]

654
00:46:12.985 --> 00:46:15.510
-...so do you mind picking her up?
-I can totally pick her up.

655
00:46:15.688 --> 00:46:19.852
KATE: Hi. How are you?
No, just eating dinner. You?

656
00:46:21.294 --> 00:46:24.229
-Who was that?
-Oh, that's her new boyfriend, Taylor.

657
00:46:24.397 --> 00:46:26.024
What? She has a boyfriend?

658
00:46:26.199 --> 00:46:29.396
They go to Borders together and read books.
And it's really dorky.

659
00:46:29.569 --> 00:46:31.127
-Do you wanna see a picture?
-Yes.

660
00:46:31.304 --> 00:46:33.033
ANNA:
Hand me the bag, please.

661
00:46:33.206 --> 00:46:36.073
SARA:
The chicken. Watch the chicken, please.

662
00:46:37.210 --> 00:46:39.405
He's actually a really good kid. I like him.

663
00:46:40.513 --> 00:46:41.775
KELLY:
Thank you.

664
00:46:41.948 --> 00:46:43.176
Oh, wow.

665
00:46:43.349 --> 00:46:45.647
He's, like, really good.

666
00:46:46.018 --> 00:46:48.509
SARA: Cute, right?
-Yeah.

667
00:46:48.688 --> 00:46:49.712
Hey!

668
00:46:50.156 --> 00:46:51.919
You did good, Katie!

669
00:46:56.062 --> 00:46:59.190
[CAR APPROACHES]

670
00:46:59.365 --> 00:47:01.833
[DOGS BARKlNG]

671
00:47:06.806 --> 00:47:09.331
BRlAN: What's the matter?
-Nothing.

672
00:47:09.509 --> 00:47:11.136
She's home.

673
00:48:08.901 --> 00:48:10.892
I can taste your Cytoxan.

674
00:48:12.038 --> 00:48:13.630
I'm sorry.

675
00:48:13.806 --> 00:48:17.139
No, it's okay.

676
00:48:18.411 --> 00:48:20.345
I kind of like it.

677
00:48:21.614 --> 00:48:23.479
Besides...

678
00:48:24.450 --> 00:48:26.941
...they told me yesterday I'm relapsing.

679
00:48:27.119 --> 00:48:28.882
I start chemo next week.

680
00:48:29.722 --> 00:48:32.088
Maybe it'll save me a dose.

681
00:49:18.437 --> 00:49:20.530
I had a really good time.

682
00:49:21.073 --> 00:49:22.836
Me too.

683
00:49:47.333 --> 00:49:49.198
KATE:
Are you awake?

684
00:49:51.037 --> 00:49:53.062
I am now.

685
00:49:54.740 --> 00:49:57.800
So how was it? Was it great?

686
00:49:57.977 --> 00:49:59.137
We kissed.

687
00:49:59.779 --> 00:50:01.644
You kissed?

688
00:50:02.314 --> 00:50:05.010
What was it like?
Was it sexy? Was it gross?

689
00:50:05.184 --> 00:50:08.312
-Was there a lot of tongue?
-No.

690
00:50:09.655 --> 00:50:11.418
It was nice.

691
00:50:11.590 --> 00:50:13.854
How did you know what to do?

692
00:50:14.026 --> 00:50:15.618
I don't know.

693
00:50:15.795 --> 00:50:17.660
I just did.

694
00:50:26.172 --> 00:50:28.970
He has scars on his hands...

695
00:50:30.609 --> 00:50:33.100
...from graft-versus-host.

696
00:50:33.946 --> 00:50:37.279
I could feel them
when we were holding hands.

697
00:50:37.583 --> 00:50:38.880
Was that weird?

698
00:50:40.386 --> 00:50:43.082
It was kind of like we matched.

699
00:50:51.664 --> 00:50:53.222
Hello.

700
00:50:53.399 --> 00:50:55.390
-Hi.
-Hey.

701
00:50:59.138 --> 00:51:00.867
How far into it are you?

702
00:51:01.040 --> 00:51:03.133
Just started.

703
00:51:05.644 --> 00:51:09.102
A hundred bucks says you won't make it
till 3 without tossing your cookies.

704
00:51:09.281 --> 00:51:11.306
KATE:
You're disgusting.

705
00:51:12.051 --> 00:51:14.144
Such a slacker.
Don't you have anywhere to be?

706
00:51:14.320 --> 00:51:15.981
No, nowhere. And what are you doing?

707
00:51:16.155 --> 00:51:18.350
You're trying to weasel your way
out of the bet?

708
00:51:18.524 --> 00:51:21.084
I'm trying to spare you. Although--

709
00:51:24.530 --> 00:51:25.997
TAYLOR:
Okay.

710
00:51:26.432 --> 00:51:28.127
Okay.

711
00:51:44.650 --> 00:51:46.117
Here.

712
00:51:52.191 --> 00:51:53.385
Sorry.

713
00:51:53.559 --> 00:51:56.687
What are you talking about?
Tomorrow it could be me. Here.

714
00:52:40.139 --> 00:52:41.367
No.

715
00:52:41.540 --> 00:52:43.667
Trust me, you need it.

716
00:52:43.843 --> 00:52:45.435
Trust me.

717
00:53:02.394 --> 00:53:04.794
-Fun date, huh?
-Hm.

718
00:53:07.533 --> 00:53:10.969
-So you had French fries for lunch, huh?
-You're disgusting.

719
00:53:11.136 --> 00:53:13.331
And you lost the bet, pay up.

720
00:53:13.505 --> 00:53:17.134
I don't have any money.
I'll pay you in sexual favors.

721
00:53:17.309 --> 00:53:18.799
[CHUCKLES]

722
00:53:19.445 --> 00:53:21.140
That's-- That's just great.

723
00:53:21.313 --> 00:53:23.804
Why don't we ask your mom about that?

724
00:53:23.983 --> 00:53:28.215
Keep it up and your next date
will be during a bone-marrow aspiration.

725
00:53:28.654 --> 00:53:29.951
Just joking.

726
00:53:30.122 --> 00:53:32.522
Yeah, I hope so.

727
00:53:33.525 --> 00:53:36.323
